---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import VerifiedLightingRoutes from '../../components/VerifiedLightingRoutes.astro';
---
<ContentLayout title="Small bedroom gaming setup | StackGeist" description="A practical small-bedroom gaming setup strategy built around floor space, desk depth, storage, lighting, and shared-use constraints." active="setups">
  <article class="article-page">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/setups/gaming">Gaming setups</a> / Small spaces</p>
    <p class="page-kicker">Small spaces</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">A small gaming room needs fewer collisions, not smaller versions of everything.</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">Protect walking space, door clearance, chair movement, and desk depth first. Then use vertical space and selective lighting to make the room feel intentional.</p>
    <div class="page-actions"><a class="btn" href="#verified-lighting">See verified lighting picks ↓</a></div>

    <div class="article-summary">
      <strong>Design target</strong>
      <p>One clear path through the room, one usable desk surface, and storage that goes upward instead of spreading across the floor.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="article-body">
      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Measure the collisions</p>
        <h2>Start with the space the chair and doors need.</h2>
        <p>The desk footprint is only part of the setup. A chair needs room to pull back. Closet and room doors need to open. Drawers need clearance. A bed edge may turn a narrow aisle into a dead end.</p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Mark where the chair sits during use and where it moves when you stand up.</li>
          <li>Open every nearby door, drawer, or closet before choosing the desk position.</li>
          <li>Check power and Ethernet access before committing to the wall that photographs best.</li>
          <li>Keep vents, heaters, and equipment ventilation paths clear.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Desk strategy</p>
        <h2>Spend depth carefully.</h2>
        <p>In a tight bedroom, an unnecessarily deep desk can consume the aisle while a shallow desk can make the monitor, keyboard, and mouse compete for the same space. The right depth is the one that supports the display position and input space you need without stealing room from circulation.</p>
        <div class="article-cards">
          <div><strong>Wall run</strong><p>Usually simplest. Keeps cables and lighting concentrated on one wall and preserves the rest of the room.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Corner use</strong><p>Useful when two short wall sections are available, but check chair rotation and avoid creating a cable trap in the corner.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Shared-use desk</strong><p>If the desk also handles school or work, preserve one open zone instead of dedicating the entire surface to gaming gear.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Go vertical</p>
        <h2>Move storage off the floor and off the primary desk surface.</h2>
        <p>Headsets, spare controllers, handhelds, boxes, and accessories do not all need permanent desk space. Shelving or wall storage can help, but keep frequently touched items reachable and do not create a wall of objects just because vertical space exists.</p>
        <div class="note">Vertical storage is valuable when it returns usable floor or desk area. It is not automatically better if it makes cleaning, charging, or reaching gear harder.</div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Lighting</p>
        <h2>Use one controlled light source before adding several accent lights.</h2>
        <p>Small rooms reflect light quickly. One task light or soft bias light can create separation around the desk without adding multiple lamps, strips, and visible power leads. The room usually looks cleaner when lighting has one job.</p>
        <VerifiedLightingRoutes premiumFit="Best when a small desk needs useful work light without adding a lamp base." budgetFit="Best when you only want monitor backlight and want to keep the upgrade inexpensive." />
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Build order</p>
        <ol class="article-steps">
          <li><strong>Choose the wall.</strong><span>Base it on clearance, power, ventilation, and daily movement.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Protect chair space.</strong><span>Make sure the setup still works when you need to stand up or move across the room.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Place the essential gear.</strong><span>Display, inputs, PC or console, and required audio first.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Route power and cables.</strong><span>Do this before adding storage or decoration that blocks access.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Add only the storage you need.</strong><span>Move infrequent items upward or elsewhere.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Add lighting last.</strong><span>Use it to support the setup, not to hide a crowded layout.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="related-reading">
        <div class="cards">
          <a class="card" href="/setups/midnight-shift"><h3>Midnight Shift</h3><p>A dark mixed-use setup designed around a smaller room and a protected desk surface.</p></a>
          <a class="card" href="/guides/desk-layout-basics"><h3>Desk layout basics</h3><p>Work out the primary zone before deciding where accessories belong.</p></a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</ContentLayout>
